  "name": "Zār (Egyptian spirit-possession ritual music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Women-led ritual of spirit possession and healing practised in Cairo and across Egypt, in which leaders called kodia sing to the spirits over large jingled frame drums and the goblet-shaped tabla. Egyptian practice combines several strands, including a Sudanese-derived tanbura style with the bowl lyre and the manjur rattle belt of goat hooves; the Mazaher ensemble, based at Makan (Egyptian Center for Culture and Art) in Cairo, has brought it to the concert stage.",
